# TulipFarm Local Development Environment

# Datastore — the bundled pgvector/pgvector:pg17 container (docker-compose.dev.yml).
# Postgres is the single V1 datastore (pgvector + pg-boss).
# setup-dev.sh rewrites this value with the generated POSTGRES_PASSWORD from .env.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://tulipfarm@localhost:5432/tulipfarm

# Soul repo path — MUST be an absolute path to a dedicated directory OUTSIDE this project, so the
# soul's own git commits never touch the project repo. setup-dev.sh expands this to ~/.tulipfarm/soul.
# (A relative/in-repo path is rejected at boot — see GitSyncService.ensureRepo.)
# Local dev always uses SOUL_PATH (one global soul checkout). Hosted/production deployments with
# per-business soul repos use SOUL_ROOT instead (resolveSoulPath derives <root>/<businessId>/soul);
# exactly one of SOUL_PATH/SOUL_ROOT is required at boot.
SOUL_PATH=~/.tulipfarm/soul

# Bootstrap Env Config
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
ENCRYPTION_KEY=<generate: openssl rand -base64 32>
JWT_SECRET=<generate: openssl rand -base64 32>
WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET=<generate: openssl rand -base64 32>

# Session auth (issue #7)
# Bootstrap admin: bypasses the setup wizard — only set these for headless/CI deployments.
# Leave commented to use the first-run setup wizard in the browser.
# ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@tulipfarm.dev
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=<set a strong password>
# Session cookie TTL in seconds (default 604800 = 7 days)
SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=604800

# Authorization enforcement (docs/architecture/authorization-design.md §5 step 2).
# Default (unset) is `enforcing`: the decision engine's answer is served. Set `shadow` to rehearse
# a policy change — the engine still runs, but each route falls back to its declared admin/member
# check, so nothing new can be refused. Either way, every disagreement between the two is logged as
# `authz.divergence`. Run in shadow until that log is quiet, then unset it.
# AUTHZ_MODE=shadow

# Public-origin fallbacks for headless/IaC installs. Admins can otherwise set the public address in
# Business → About. Integration OAuth callbacks use the API origin and must be registered verbatim.
# Defaults: http://localhost:4000 and http://localhost:4010.
# PUBLIC_URL=https://tulip.example.com
# PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.tulip.example.com
# Set true for environment-only/IaC deployments; the in-app fields become read-only.
# PUBLIC_ORIGINS_LOCKED=true

# Optional: Git-backed soul repo (set after first run to enable remote sync)
# SOUL_GIT_REMOTE_URL=https://github.com/yourname/soul.git
# SOUL_GIT_CREDENTIAL=ghp_xxxx

# Optional: Published bundle retention window in days (default 30, minimum 7)
# SOUL_BUNDLE_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Optional: Key rotation (set only when rotating ENCRYPTION_KEY)
# ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS=<old key during rotation>

# Optional: API-token pepper. When set, API-token hashes use HMAC-SHA256(token, pepper) instead of
# bare SHA-256, so a database dump alone can't verify token guesses. Legacy tokens keep working and
# upgrade lazily on next use. ONE-WAY DOOR: once tokens upgrade, removing/changing this value (e.g.
# a deploy rollback) breaks them — set it once and keep it stable, or re-issue tokens.
# API_TOKEN_PEPPER=<generate: openssl rand -base64 32>

# Secrets are encrypted under a Data Encryption Key (DEK) that is itself wrapped under ENCRYPTION_KEY.
# On first boot the DEK is auto-provisioned (zero-setup). To survive loss of ENCRYPTION_KEY, mint an
# offline recovery key once and store it safely:
#   pnpm --filter @tulipfarm/api keys show-recovery
# Recovery / rotation runbook: docs/runbooks/secrets-key-recovery.md

# Optional: Port configuration (defaults shown)
# PORT=4010
# VITE_PORT=4000

# Durable worker (@tulipfarm/worker, `pnpm dev:worker`). It shares DATABASE_URL with the API and
# never migrates — it refuses to start below the schema version the API has applied.
#
# It executes turns, so it also needs two things the API does not have to give it a URL for:
#   * INTERNAL_API_URL — where the API's internal turn host answers. Required.
#   * WORKER_API_CREDENTIAL — a service API client secret (`tfc_<clientId>.<secret>`). Left empty
#     here on purpose: `setup-dev.sh` sets TF_DATA_DIR, so the API auto-mints this on first boot
#     (apps/api/src/setup/worker-credential.ts) and the worker reads it back from the same data
#     dir (apps/worker/src/data-dir.ts) — no manual step. loadConfig only checks the var is
#     non-empty, so pasting a placeholder string here would boot the worker cleanly while every
#     internal API call then failed auth — chat creates a Run and silently never replies, with no
#     error logged anywhere. Only set this yourself to pin a credential you minted by hand
#     (Settings → API clients does not exist in the web UI yet; use
#     `POST /api/v1/identity/api-clients` with an admin session).
# It also reads ENCRYPTION_KEY (above) — the same value the API uses — to unwrap the provider API
# keys named by the Soul's llm.config.yaml. A different key means it cannot decrypt them.
INTERNAL_API_URL=http://localhost:4010
WORKER_API_CREDENTIAL=
#
# Defaults shown below.
# WORKER_PORT=4020              # /livez + /readyz
# WORKER_OWNER=<hostname>:<pid> # lease owner; must be unique per process
# WORKER_RUN_POLL_MS=1000
# WORKER_WAIT_SWEEP_MS=5000
# WORKER_OUTBOX_POLL_MS=1000
# WORKER_BATCH_SIZE=25
# WORKER_LEASE_MS=60000         # must exceed WORKER_RUN_POLL_MS
# WORKER_RUN_MAX_LIFETIME_MS=900000 # max a Run may renew its lease before reclaim; must exceed WORKER_LEASE_MS
# WORKER_DRAIN_TIMEOUT_MS=15000 # SIGTERM budget; a timed-out drain exits non-zero
# Local dev is a single replica, so it must own the cron/maintenance consumers. Left off, the
# task reconciler never runs and the Tasks list stays permanently empty — which reads as a broken
# product rather than a missing flag. Set to true on exactly one replica when running several.
WORKER_MAINTENANCE=true
# Development only: exact immutable image used for published Skill shell/TypeScript/Python Tools.
# Production ignores this local-container backend until an attested remote backend is configured.
# SANDBOX_RUNTIME_IMAGE=ghcr.io/tulipfarm/skill-runtime@sha256:<64 lowercase hex characters>

# Integration worker (@tulipfarm/integration-worker, `pnpm dev:integration-worker`). Slack/Telegram
# ingress — same shape as the durable worker above, and needs the same two things:
#   * INTERNAL_API_URL — where the API's internal turn host answers. Required.
#   * INTEGRATION_WORKER_API_CREDENTIAL — a service API client secret (`tfc_<clientId>.<secret>`).
#     Left empty for the same reason as WORKER_API_CREDENTIAL above — auto-minted via TF_DATA_DIR.
INTERNAL_API_URL=http://localhost:4010
INTEGRATION_WORKER_API_CREDENTIAL=
#
# Defaults shown below.
# INTEGRATION_WORKER_PORT=4030              # /livez + /readyz
# INTEGRATION_WORKER_DRAIN_TIMEOUT_MS=15000 # SIGTERM budget; a timed-out drain exits non-zero

# The Curator's five-minute loop records its effects in shadow and applies them to nothing —
# read them at `GET /api/v1/curator/shadow`.

# File storage. Unset, uploads and generated documents land on disk under TF_DATA_DIR — right for
# development, wrong for anything with more than one replica or a container that ever restarts.
# Point it at any S3-compatible bucket by filling these in; naming a bucket is enough to select it.
# Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, Wasabi, DigitalOcean Spaces, Ceph and MinIO all work
# through the same six settings — there is no vendor-specific option, by design. Azure Blob and
# Google Cloud Storage are reached through their own S3-compatible endpoints.
# BLOB_STORE=s3                             # inferred from S3_BUCKET; set it to force one way
# S3_BUCKET=tulipfarm
# S3_REGION=us-east-1                       # every provider signs with one, even single-region ones
# S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000         # omit for Amazon S3 itself
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true                  # defaults on whenever S3_ENDPOINT is set
# S3_PREFIX=files                           # share one bucket with something else
#
# The compose stack instead ships its own S3 server (`bucket`), so an install needs no cloud
# account. This is what selects it: the API provisions the bucket and its access key over the
# server's admin API on first boot, then persists them to `bucket.env` beside the other generated
# secrets. Nothing sets it for local development, where disk is the right answer.
# BUCKET_ADMIN_URL=http://bucket:3903
